WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:08.560
Some say that SCP-2102 was a mistake, an abomination
created by an accident of medical science.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.400
Maybe those people are right, maybe there’s
much more to it than that.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.200
Either way, they don’t know the full story.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.700
How could they?

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.230
They weren’t there all the way back in 1972.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:24.240
But Doctor Pavel Zukal was.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.390
The earliest moments, the events that directly
preceded SCP-2102’s creation began on March

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:31.390
2nd.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:36.519
The wind was carrying an unforgiving, freezing
bite with it as Zukal made his way to the

00:00:36.519 --> 00:00:40.989
Institute of Experimental Medicine ČSAV in
Czechoslovakia.

00:00:40.989 --> 00:00:45.300
With the Cold War between the USSR and the
United States underway, there was already

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.710
an air of secrecy on both sides.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:53.059
Espionage and intelligence-gathering operations
were rampant, bringing with them paranoia

00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:54.059
and distrust.

00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:58.870
There was no way of knowing if someone was
a double agent leaking information to the

00:00:58.870 --> 00:00:59.870
West.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.969
But while the governments of Russia, America,
and their various respective allies and enemies

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:09.070
scrambled for control and amassed obscene
quantities of nuclear arms in secret, there

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:14.660
were even more cogs turning, hidden in the
deepest, darkest shadows.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:19.710
The SCP Foundation’s long-standing rivalry
with various other Groups of Interest was

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.380
well-known among any with a high enough security
clearance.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:28.150
Most of these groups were radicals or outliers;
despite possessing knowledge of anomalies

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:33.640
or even being directly linked to them, a lot
of GOIs were independent of any world government.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:39.880
But, for decades, the Foundation had been
aware of a directly competing establishment,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.420
effectively a parallel of their own organization
on the other side of the globe.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:50.950
They were known as GRU Division P, a USSR
equivalent of the SCP Foundation themselves.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.630
It's unclear, based on what was recovered
from Doctor Zukal’s personal diary, if he

00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:00.850
had been directly scouted by GRU Division
P for their project in Czechoslovakia.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.980
Given the air of secrecy engulfing the USSR
at the time, it’s possible that Pavel himself

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:11.230
had no inclination that Division P was even
involved in the project due to take place

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.580
at the Institute of Experimental Medicine.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.150
He might have suspected, but with no way of
knowing for sure.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:21.500
Similarly, there was no way Zukal could have
known what the project would lead…

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.330
what he’d have a hand in creating.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:29.250
The initial few weeks after his arrival at
the Institute were rocky, to say the least.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.760
Zukal, along with a team of scientists and
technicians working on orders direct from

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.070
Moscow, had to contend with unreliable equipment
and difficulty getting electricity to power

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:40.070
the project.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:44.190
Eventually, after clearing those initial hurdles,
testing could begin.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.510
But what was the goal of this mysterious project?

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:53.350
Well, through Zukal, GRU Division P and the
Kremlin were chasing something the scientific

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:57.060
world has been trying to accomplish for decades.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.690
Something that could change the very landscape
of not only the Cold War but the entirety

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.290
of modern medicine: the secrets of cellular
regeneration.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:10.910
Unlocking that would, in theory, enable them
to create the world’s very first unkillable

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:16.090
super-soldier - and hey, it definitely wouldn’t
be the craziest secret operation carried out

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:18.650
by a world superpower during the Cold War.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:23.790
The CIA spent millions turning a cat into
a listening device and training it to infiltrate

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.450
enemy headquarters.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:30.680
This so-called “Acoustic Kitty” was struck
and killed by a random van during its first

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:31.680
mission.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.990
Compared to that, a cell-regenerating super
soldier was positively sensible.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:41.099
With a worldwide nuclear arms race already
underway, perhaps the project’s ultimate

00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:45.440
goal was to produce an entire army of these
self-regenerating troops.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.650
With something like that in their hands, the
USSR would have the manpower to reinforce

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.870
their nuclear arsenal, and potentially even
soldiers capable of surviving and healing

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:57.650
from the effects of an atomic blast.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:02.830
On the fifth of May 1972, the first of the
project’s human test subjects began to show

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:04.380
promising results.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.090
They underwent physical trauma at the hands
of Zukal and the other scientists, only for

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:13.800
their bodies to react much faster than the
ordinary healing process would allow.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.069
Rapid clotting took place in the affected
areas, preventing the subjects from bleeding

00:04:18.069 --> 00:04:19.069
out.

00:04:19.069 --> 00:04:23.669
However, this quickly went awry, the clots
turning into severe lesions.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:28.389
Realizing this had occurred thanks to Michail
– one of the other project scientists – administering

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:34.379
too much neodymium, Zukal chastised his colleague,
vowing to drag him to Moscow personally if

00:04:34.379 --> 00:04:37.279
another mistake like that ever took place
again.

00:04:37.279 --> 00:04:42.849
Unfortunately, by the last few days of June,
Doctor Zukal and his team were no closer to

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:47.400
success; every subject that underwent testing
succumbed to the same necrosis.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:52.169
Reducing the amount of neodymium had only
made the reaction worse, something Pavel couldn’t

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:56.330
allow Michail to know without the risk of
it being held over his head for the remainder

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:57.569
of the project.

00:04:57.569 --> 00:05:02.969
In every instance, the doctors were able to
trigger rapid cell division, fifty times faster

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:06.639
than that which occurs in the ordinary healing
process of human beings.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:12.280
But the biggest drawback was that this division
would quickly become uncontrollable.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:17.229
It was creating enough new matter on a cellular
level, that it would choke each test subject

00:05:17.229 --> 00:05:19.500
in its own excess waste.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:25.309
As you might expect, Doctor Zukal noted in
his diary that this process wasn’t a pretty

00:05:25.309 --> 00:05:26.309
sight.

00:05:26.309 --> 00:05:31.479
Come mid-August 1972, the project scientists
decided they needed a test subject with more

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:36.279
resilience than the average person, and a
suitable candidate was provided.

00:05:36.279 --> 00:05:40.770
He was a counter-revolutionary, who had been
sentenced to fifteen years in a uranium mine

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:42.770
as a punishment detail.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.860
Despite such an extended period of time under
the harsh conditions of the mine, and the

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:53.430
close proximity to a radioactive element such
as uranium, the man managed to survive unscathed.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:58.869
Upon closer examination, this new guinea pig
– referred to as X-16 – had a rate of

00:05:58.869 --> 00:06:02.550
healing twice as fast as that of the average
man.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:07.580
Perhaps it was a random genetic abnormality
that made X-16 this way, or maybe it was a

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.949
result of his extensive time in the uranium
mines.

00:06:10.949 --> 00:06:15.470
Either way, it made him the perfect new subject
for Zukal’s experiments.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:20.389
Initially, Subject X-16’s preliminary testing
results looked promising, the wound healing

00:06:20.389 --> 00:06:25.469
process was actually taking place now, instead
of resulting in necrosis or aborting as it

00:06:25.469 --> 00:06:27.680
had with the earlier experiments.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:33.090
In fact, Doctor Zukal seemed to think X-16
was healing almost too quickly.

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:35.800
But, nevertheless, testing continued.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.620
Although not without a different type of problem.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:43.320
The questionable morality of what they were
doing began to set in for Pavel, causing him

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:48.729
nights of endless tossing and turning, unable
to sleep as he reflected on what he’d done.

00:06:48.729 --> 00:06:53.389
X-16 had managed to survive for much longer
than the earlier test subjects, but it was

00:06:53.389 --> 00:06:57.759
clear that the torture this man had to endure
was exacting a heavy toll.

00:06:57.759 --> 00:07:02.909
Every day, the doctors would lacerate him,
time and time again, recording the results.

00:07:02.909 --> 00:07:07.680
And every day, he survived, only for the cycle
to begin again the next day.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.629
For his own part, Zukal was conflicted.

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:15.120
On the one hand, the results of the testing
were proving successful, lasting far longer

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.430
and working far more effectively than they
had in the past.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:23.479
And yet, on the other, it came at the cost
of one man’s continued suffering.

00:07:23.479 --> 00:07:28.439
To make matters worse, Pavel had no way of
voicing his concerns to any of his team.

00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:32.800
Any questioning of his orders or the project’s
methods would have been viewed by GRU Division

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:39.119
P and the KGB as sedition and treason, earning
the good doctor a one-way ticket to a gulag

00:07:39.119 --> 00:07:44.389
or, if he was lucky, a clean shot to the back
of the head from an agent’s pistol.

00:07:44.389 --> 00:07:49.289
Often, morality is sacrificed for the greater
good, in the pursuit of the advancement of

00:07:49.289 --> 00:07:51.449
science or a nation’s goals.

00:07:51.449 --> 00:07:56.960
But, now that the project was showing results
with X-16, now that it was real, what they

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.979
were doing still weighed heavily on Doctor
Zukal’s conscience.

00:08:00.979 --> 00:08:07.550
Then, on October 15th, 1972, the final piece
of the puzzle seemed to become clear.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:12.740
By introducing the correct amount of a stabilizing
agent into the serum they had concocted, Zukal

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.979
and his team had perfected the composition.

00:08:15.979 --> 00:08:20.909
Upon administering this newly completed version
of the solution, the doctor noted in his diary

00:08:20.909 --> 00:08:24.089
that Subject X-16 was responding well.

00:08:24.089 --> 00:08:28.069
At least, medically the subject was responding
well.

00:08:28.069 --> 00:08:33.289
It was clear that the continuous experimentation
he had been subjected to was causing uncooperative

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:34.530
behavior in X-16.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:40.740
But, so close to the cusp of achieving the
project’s goals, Doctor Zukal was quick

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:44.560
to cast aside his previous doubts about the
ethics of their methods.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:49.850
They were so close to victory, so close to
the project being over, that it no longer

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:53.050
mattered how cooperative X-16 was being.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:58.490
But, two weeks and four days later, just as
the project was nearing its end goal, disaster

00:08:58.490 --> 00:08:59.490
struck.

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:04.699
MU Doctor Elena Oravcova was one of the scientists
on Zukal’s team, who had primarily helped

00:09:04.699 --> 00:09:09.509
when examining the early cell divisions, those
that were leading to necrosis and causing

00:09:09.509 --> 00:09:13.279
test subjects to produce too much excessive
genetic material.

00:09:13.279 --> 00:09:18.410
Elena had made a startling discovery and immediately
brought it to Doctor Zukal in a distraught

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:19.899
show of panic.

00:09:19.899 --> 00:09:25.110
The cell samples they had used to test their
serum were now gaining mass, seemingly from

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:26.170
nowhere.

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:31.690
Just like the earlier experiments, the effects
were working almost too well, causing an overabundance

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:35.820
of cellular regeneration to the point where
the cells couldn’t stop producing masses

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:37.370
of waste genetic material.

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:43.660
When Zukal brought Elena’s findings to Doctor
Szilard and one Jurij T. Fedenko – the project

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:48.750
heads assigned by Division P – they were
quick to disregard it as an error, determined

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:51.519
to press on with the project to meet their
deadline.

00:09:51.519 --> 00:09:56.269
Over the course of the following weeks, up
to late November, Zukal was trying to determine

00:09:56.269 --> 00:09:58.360
what was causing this change.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:02.930
It was clear that, unless he and Elena could
resolve the issues with the serum, Subject

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.520
X-16 would be a total failure.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:10.339
But time was running out, with their superiors
at GRU Division P and the Kremlin expecting

00:10:10.339 --> 00:10:12.430
results by early December.

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:18.110
Under untold amounts of pressure and subsequent
stress, Doctor Pavel Zukal found himself losing

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:22.080
time, unable to sleep to the point he was
drifting off whenever he was on the verge

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:23.190
of a breakthrough.

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:27.190
By the end, Elena was the only member of the
team trying to help him methodically figure

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.100
out what had gone wrong.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:33.000
The deadline for the project arrived on December
2nd, 1972.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:37.200
Pavel didn’t sleep all night leading up
to it, knowing he would have to present a

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:42.420
functioning super soldier to GRU Division
P. In the best case, he was risking being

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:47.680
accused of negligent failure, forced to restart
the project from scratch, making his months

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:49.190
of hard work redundant.

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:53.930
But, worst-case scenario, Zukal could be charged
with treason for having not completed the

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:56.029
task he was selected for.

00:10:56.029 --> 00:10:59.730
On the second of December, he wrote:
“I really do not know which is better.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:02.480
Eh, tomorrow is a day too.”

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.430
Nobody knows if Doctor Pavel Zukal lived to
see that tomorrow; his final diary entry was

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:11.490
made on the day SCP-2102 was born.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:16.860
For months, the SCP Foundation had operatives
embedded within the Czechoslovakian government,

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:21.190
as part of innumerable Cold War espionage
gathering efforts.

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:25.180
During that time, their agents had gathered
enough intelligence to make the Foundation

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:31.151
aware of a GRU Division P project based at
the Institute of Experimental Medicine, and

00:11:31.151 --> 00:11:36.279
that the scientists there were looking to
unlock the secret of rapid cellular regeneration.

00:11:36.279 --> 00:11:41.699
Someone, possibly even one of Zukal’s team,
had managed to infiltrate the project and

00:11:41.699 --> 00:11:47.360
had leaked documents to the SCP Foundation,
explaining what was going on in Czechoslovakia.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:51.490
The plan was that the Foundation would move
in – in secret, of course – to extract

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:57.069
and contain test subject X-16 in January of
1973.

00:11:57.069 --> 00:11:59.420
That day would never come to fruition though.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:03.779
On the second of December, only a month before
they had planned to act, the Foundation became

00:12:03.779 --> 00:12:07.709
aware of a catastrophe occurring at the Institute
of Experimental Medicine.

00:12:07.709 --> 00:12:12.449
One of their agents in Czechoslovakia alerted
them to a disturbance that had caused civilians

00:12:12.449 --> 00:12:17.759
and scientists alike to run from the Institute
in fear for their lives.

00:12:17.759 --> 00:12:21.779
Something had gotten loose, and the Foundation
had to act fast.

00:12:21.779 --> 00:12:25.860
Luckily, a reconnaissance team was stationed
in Eastern Europe, members of various Mobile

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:30.970
Task Forces who had been spying on GRU Division
P and the rest of the USSR.

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:34.680
Moving quickly, the recon team was deployed
to Czechoslovakia.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:38.170
Led by a field commander named Merrick, they
were given the objective to determine the

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:43.199
cause of the situation and, if possible, recover
its anomalous source.

00:12:43.199 --> 00:12:46.920
Merrick and the recon team arrived on-site
at the Institute’s grounds.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:51.079
The entrance to the bunker which had been
housing the project was left wide open, with

00:12:51.079 --> 00:12:52.820
not a soul in sight.

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:57.199
As the team progressed further, Merrick couldn’t
help but notice a pungent smell permeating

00:12:57.199 --> 00:12:59.960
the labs and offices that they passed.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:04.240
Foundation Command urged their operatives
to proceed cautiously, and recover any paperwork

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:05.440
they could.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:10.131
Among the logistical documents was a diary,
belonging to one of the scientists; although

00:13:10.131 --> 00:13:15.259
the name ‘Pavel Zukal’ on the cover was
no longer legible, forever lost.

00:13:15.259 --> 00:13:19.199
The further he and the other MTF agents progressed,
the worse the smell got.

00:13:19.199 --> 00:13:24.480
They located an elevator, deciding to take
it down to the lower levels of the bunker.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:27.980
One by one, the team filed in, Merrick radioing
Command.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:31.100
“Command, any seismic activity in this area?”
he asked.

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:34.380
“Not that we know of, Merrick,” came the
Foundation’s reply.

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:35.380
“Why?”

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:38.670
“I think I just felt something move down
there…”

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:43.319
The elevator had barely descended a foot before
it hit something, stopping it in an instant.

00:13:43.319 --> 00:13:48.050
As Merrick tried once again to contact Foundation
Command, that same something came tearing

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:50.139
through the elevator floor.

00:13:50.139 --> 00:13:55.459
Over the radio, the SCP Foundation were treated
to a cacophony of screeching, tearing metal,

00:13:55.459 --> 00:13:57.810
along with the screams of their own men.

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:02.009
What Merrick and his team had encountered
may have once been a man, perhaps someone

00:14:02.009 --> 00:14:04.330
subjected to cruelty and torture.

00:14:04.330 --> 00:14:07.800
But now, all they saw was an amorphous, fleshy
mass.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:13.459
Immediately, the recon team opened fire, engaging
what they assumed was a raging monster.

00:14:13.459 --> 00:14:18.509
It probably never occurred to them it might
have just wanted to be free, after years of

00:14:18.509 --> 00:14:22.199
imprisonment, followed by months of horrendous
experiments.

00:14:22.199 --> 00:14:25.920
Minutes later, Merrick was able to get in
contact with Command again, locked in a heated

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:27.420
battle with the entity.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:33.230
But every round fired, every shot their guns
landed, nothing was having any effect.

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:38.220
Whenever Merrick dealt damage to the creature,
part of it seemed to grow, faster and faster

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:39.579
each time.

00:14:39.579 --> 00:14:44.519
Their guns were useless, unable to stop the
expanding, monstrous test subject.

00:14:44.519 --> 00:14:48.870
Even grenades thrown by the MTF operatives,
causing multiple explosions, did little to

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:51.579
stop the entity in its tracks.

00:14:51.579 --> 00:14:56.029
Listening in over the radio, knowing they
had few options left, the SCP Foundation dispatched

00:14:56.029 --> 00:15:03.589
a pair of F-4 Phantoms, long-range supersonic
fighter jets, equipped with a payload of M47A1

00:15:03.589 --> 00:15:05.310
Napalm Incendiary bombs.

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:09.300
With Merrick and his men almost on the ropes,
Command issued an order to retreat.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:13.910
They fled the bunker, only to feel the ground
shake beneath their feet; the monster had

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:16.610
breached the topsoil, still coming after them.

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:20.660
Jones – one of the Foundation’s agents
– was pulled under the encroaching shapeless

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:23.940
mass, crushed to death as it continued its
pursuit.

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:28.459
Utterly horrified at the sight and speed of
the creature, Merrick started begging Command

00:15:28.459 --> 00:15:31.350
over the radio to get its fighter jets to
the scene.

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:35.769
Six minutes later, the sound of twin turbine
engines shot through the air, followed by

00:15:35.769 --> 00:15:38.509
the whistle of bombs careening down towards
the ground.

00:15:38.509 --> 00:15:44.649
In a second, Merrick, his team, and the creature
were engulfed in a fiery explosion, the heat

00:15:44.649 --> 00:15:48.899
proving enough to cauterize the fleshy abomination’s
expanding tissue.

00:15:48.899 --> 00:15:57.809
Ever since December 1972, the entity now designated
as SCP-2102 has been housed at Site-122, contained

00:15:57.809 --> 00:15:59.040
by the SCP Foundation.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:04.790
They keep it in a modified cell, restrained
at all times with its hands and feet in padded

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:05.790
sleeves.

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:10.220
This is done in order to prevent the risk
of injury, either accidental or intentional

00:16:10.220 --> 00:16:16.180
given both SCP-2102’s anomalous properties,
and its self-destructive tendencies.

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:20.649
Its body is covered in scars, both from its
previous escape attempts – back when it

00:16:20.649 --> 00:16:26.660
was known as Subject X-16 – and from burns
from the F-4 Phantom’s napalm payload.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:34.120
These particular injuries seared the entity’s
ears, mouth and eyes closed, rendering it

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:35.329
deaf, mute, and blind.

00:16:35.329 --> 00:16:40.440
If SCP-2102 ever suffers any form of external
injury, its anomalous properties will trigger.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:45.050
The creature, as you might have guessed, possessed
an advanced form of cellular healing, able

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:47.649
to regenerate from all wounds inflicted on
it.

00:16:47.649 --> 00:16:50.079
However, the process was never perfected.

00:16:50.079 --> 00:16:56.399
It still works too well, so well that SCP-2102’s
cells don’t know when to stop.

00:16:56.399 --> 00:17:01.800
Instead, they continue producing bodily tissue,
expanding its mass into every available space

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:03.009
without a halt.

00:17:03.009 --> 00:17:07.810
The process can be slowed if an obstacle is
introduced, boxing in the exponentially growing

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:08.810
tissue.

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:13.950
However, any object – or person – in its
path is eventually crushed under the mounting

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:14.950
pressure.

00:17:14.950 --> 00:17:20.660
At present, the Foundation still has no idea
how much SCP-2102’s tissue could expand,

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:24.940
potentially even posing a risk to the entire
world if left unchecked.

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:29.130
There is only one sure-fire way to stop the
creature’s healing; it can only be halted

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:32.340
by cauterizing the open wound with intense
heat.

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:35.890
It’s literally a sure-fire solution.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:42.980
SCP-2102 is a result of the good Doctor Zukal’s
work, combined with his and GRU Division P’s

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:44.900
willingness to put aside their morality.

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:50.470
They didn’t create an undying, regenerating
super-soldier for the USSR as they had intended.

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:56.350
Instead, all they accomplished was turning
a tortured man into a monster, an abomination

00:17:56.350 --> 00:18:00.070
endlessly tormented by the experiments performed
on him.

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:02.740
Not quite as useful on the battlefield, is
it?

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:09.250
Now go check out “SCP-049 THE PLAGUE DOCTOR”
and “SCP-847 THE MANNEQUIN” for more stories

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:13.350
of medically macabre monstrosities sure to
make your blood run cold.

